  "notes": "West leads the ♠Q against 3NT. South wins the opening spade lead and lacks the timing to develop the diamonds. There are 2 tricks in both spades and hearts. At least 3 tricks are available from clubs. Dummy's clubs must be developed for 2 more tricks.\n\nIt seems like South should cash the ♣K, cross to the ♥A, and hope clubs break 3-3. South has a better play that will allow the contract to be made on some of the 4-2 club breaks as well.\n\nSouth should lead the ♣K and overtake with dummy's ♣A. The ♣Q is played next. If the ♣J or ♣10 falls doubleton, North's clubs are strong enough to force out the outstanding defensive club honour. The ♥A will be an entry to the long clubs.\n\nIf clubs are 3-3, South will lose an overtrick, but the contract is safe. South concedes a club trick and can later return to dummy with the ♥A.",
